2611}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jctb.2024.01.002}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02611}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpuscula Math. 46, no. 2 (2026), 127–137 (https://www.opuscula.agh.edu.pl/vol46/2/art/opuscula_math_4606.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; recently provide a full solution to this problem.\nTheorem 1.1 (Keusch [17]). ... A very legitimate concern is to wonder about the tightness of the 1-2-3 Conjecture.\n128\nOlivier Baudon, Julien Bensmail, and Lyn Vayssieres\nLet us mention to the reader that there are more concrete interpretations behind\nall these notions, refer e.g. to [5]. On the more theoretical side of things, most of the\nrelated investigations in the field have been on the maximum value that χe\nΣ(G) can\nattain for a nice graph G. Said investigations have been mostly driven by the so-called\n1-2-3 Conjecture, introduced by Karoński, łuczak, and Thomason [16], which reads\nas follows.\n1-2-3 Conjecture. If G is a nice graph, then χe\nΣ(G) ≤3.\nSeveral aspects of the 1-2-3 Conjecture have been investigated since its introduction,\nsee e.g. [21] for some survey. Some of these investigations ultimately led Keusch to\nrecently provide a full solution to this problem.\nTheorem 1.1 (Keusch [17]). The 1-2-3 Conjecture holds true.\nAlthough the 1-2-3 Conjecture was fully verified, many of its aspects of interest\nremain to be fully understood. Such aspects include variants of the 1-2-3 Conjecture\nthat have been introduced over the years. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nliens-94-6.A4.dvi (https://www.di.ens.fr/reports/1994/liens-94-6.A4.pdf)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Testing the theory of evolution by comparing phylogenetic trees constructed from five different protein sequences. ... Winkler. Collapse of the metric hierarch